00:02:04.742 --> 00:02:05.595
Part 1.

22
00:02:05.875 --> 00:02:07.795
Introduction and Why It Matters.

23
00:02:09.045 --> 00:02:12.373
A story: we moved to virtual threads and it got slower.

24
00:02:12.653 --> 00:02:22.616
The platform team upgraded catalog-service to Java 21 and switched Tomcat to virtual threads (spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true).

25
00:02:22.896 --> 00:02:24.389
Load tests looked great:

26
00:02:24.669 --> 00:02:29.299
5,000 concurrent requests on a pod that used to top out at 200.

27
00:02:29.579 --> 00:02:30.816
Then, in production:

28
00:02:32.166 --> 00:02:38.310
The product page p99 went from 180 ms to 6 seconds at lunchtime peak.

29
00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:52.158
Thread dumps showed thousands of virtual threads waiting and only two or four carrier threads, all stuck inside a synchronized method of an old image-metadata client that did an HTTP call while holding the lock.

30
00:02:52.438 --> 00:03:01.974
On JDK 21, a virtual thread blocking inside synchronized pins its carrier, so the whole pod ran at the concurrency of its CPU count.

31
00:03:02.254 --> 00:03:03.662
L2 reproduces it:

32
00:03:03.942 --> 00:03:09.809
5,031 ms versus 53 ms for the same work with a ReentrantLock.

33
00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:13.655
Memory grew by 400 MB per pod.

34
00:03:13.935 --> 00:03:27.737
A JSON library cached a 16 KB buffer in a ThreadLocal, which is fine with 200 pooled threads (200 buffers) and a disaster with a virtual thread per request (one buffer per in-flight request).

35
00:03:28.017 --> 00:03:32.967
L2: +349 MB at 20,000 in-flight requests.

36
00:03:33.247 --> 00:03:34.761
Pricing-service fell over.

37
00:03:35.041 --> 00:03:45.025
Removing the 200-thread ceiling also removed the accidental limit on how many concurrent calls catalog made to pricing (Ch 17 L1).

38
00:03:45.305 --> 00:03:48.292
Pricing received 5x its usual concurrency.

39
00:03:49.642 --> 00:03:53.077
The product page fan-out used CompletableFuture.allOf.

40
00:03:53.357 --> 00:04:02.103
When inventory failed, the page still waited for reviews (sometimes 2 s), and the abandoned futures kept running after the response was sent.

41
00:04:02.383 --> 00:04:03.365
The fixes:

42
00:04:03.645 --> 00:04:11.111
Upgrade to JDK 24 plus (the platform is on 25), where synchronized no longer pins (L2:

43
00:04:11.391 --> 00:04:17.023
54 ms), and replace the worst synchronized I or O with ReentrantLock anyway.

44
00:04:17.303 --> 00:04:20.866
Replace per-thread caches with pooled objects or ScopedValue.

45
00:04:22.216 --> 00:04:28.403
Put explicit concurrency limits (semaphores or bulkheads) per dependency back in (Ch 18).

46
00:04:28.683 --> 00:04:35.083
Use structured concurrency for fan-out: fail fast, cancel siblings, a budget per part, nothing leaks.

47
00:04:36.433 --> 00:04:37.115
Quote:

48
00:04:37.395 --> 00:04:39.059
The thesis of this chapter.

49
00:04:39.339 --> 00:04:42.902
Concurrency in a service is a capacity-allocation problem.

50
00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:52.057
Every in-flight request holds something (a thread, a connection, memory, a slot at a dependency), and Little's law decides how many are in flight.

51
00:04:52.337 --> 00:04:54.769
Virtual threads make waiting nearly free.

52
00:04:55.049 --> 00:05:09.534
They don't make CPUs, databases or downstream services any bigger, so keep the explicit limits, remove the old assumptions (pools, pinning, ThreadLocal), and structure concurrent work so its lifetime is bounded by the request.

