99
00:07:13.961 --> 00:07:14.835
Part 2.

100
00:07:15.115 --> 00:07:16.993
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

101
00:07:18.243 --> 00:07:20.909
Intuition: billing counters at a dark store.

102
00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:22.789
Platform threads are cashiers.

103
00:07:23.069 --> 00:07:28.104
Each is expensive to hire (memory, scheduling), and there's a fixed number of counters.

104
00:07:28.384 --> 00:07:35.829
A customer who says wait, let me call my wife about the atta brand makes the cashier stand idle, and the queue behind grows.

105
00:07:36.109 --> 00:07:38.499
That's thread-per-request with blocking I or O.

106
00:07:38.779 --> 00:07:40.763
Virtual threads are token numbers.

107
00:07:41.043 --> 00:07:44.477
Customers take a token and step aside while they're on the phone.

108
00:07:44.757 --> 00:07:48.213
A small number of cashiers (carriers) serve whoever is ready.

109
00:07:48.493 --> 00:07:51.224
Thousands of customers can be in progress cheaply.

110
00:07:52.574 --> 00:08:00.211
Pinning: a customer who takes the cashier with them into the phone booth (blocking inside synchronized on JDK 21).

111
00:08:00.491 --> 00:08:02.603
The cashier is stuck until the call ends.

112
00:08:02.883 --> 00:08:08.067
ThreadLocal caches: giving every token holder their own shopping trolley for speed.

113
00:08:08.347 --> 00:08:12.379
Fine with 8 cashiers, absurd with 5,000 token holders.

114
00:08:12.659 --> 00:08:17.907
Structured concurrency: a family splits up to collect items (atta, milk, eggs).

115
00:08:18.187 --> 00:08:21.366
If the eggs are sold out, everyone stops and comes back.

116
00:08:21.646 --> 00:08:24.697
Nobody keeps shopping for an order that's already cancelled.

117
00:08:26.047 --> 00:08:32.596
Backpressure: when the packing table is full, pickers stop bringing more items instead of piling them on the floor.

118
00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:34.799
The models.

119
00:08:35.079 --> 00:08:36.423
Here is a quick table:

120
00:08:36.703 --> 00:08:39.733
Model, and How it works, and Strengths, and Weaknesses.

121
00:08:40.013 --> 00:08:42.125
Thread-per-request (platform threads).

122
00:08:42.405 --> 00:08:43.407
How it works:

123
00:08:43.687 --> 00:08:47.741
Each request gets an OS thread from a pool (Tomcat's 200).

124
00:08:48.021 --> 00:08:48.874
Strengths:

125
00:08:49.154 --> 00:08:51.522
Simple blocking code, easy debugging.

126
00:08:51.802 --> 00:08:52.783
Weaknesses:

127
00:08:53.063 --> 00:09:00.487
Threads are expensive (~1 MB stack reserve, OS scheduling), so concurrency is capped by the pool size.

128
00:09:00.767 --> 00:09:03.989
Reactive or event-loop (Reactor, WebFlux, Netty).

129
00:09:04.269 --> 00:09:05.271
How it works:

130
00:09:05.551 --> 00:09:10.522
A few threads run non-blocking pipelines; I or O completion triggers callbacks.

131
00:09:10.802 --> 00:09:11.655
Strengths:

132
00:09:11.935 --> 00:09:14.815
Huge concurrency, built-in backpressure, streaming.

133
00:09:15.095 --> 00:09:16.077
Weaknesses:

134
00:09:16.357 --> 00:09:20.709
Harder code, debugging and stack traces; everything must be non-blocking.

135
00:09:22.059 --> 00:09:24.832
Virtual threads (JDK 21 plus).

136
00:09:25.112 --> 00:09:26.115
How it works:

137
00:09:26.395 --> 00:09:33.691
Cheap threads managed by the JVM, mounted on a few carrier threads; a blocking call unmounts the virtual thread.

138
00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:34.824
Strengths:

139
00:09:35.104 --> 00:09:37.813
Blocking code with reactive-like concurrency.

140
00:09:38.093 --> 00:09:39.075
Weaknesses:

141
00:09:39.355 --> 00:09:45.093
Pinning (JDK 21 to 23), ThreadLocal assumptions, and no built-in limits.

142
00:09:46.443 --> 00:10:01.142
L1 A: holding 5,000 requests waiting on I or O cost +151 MB and 1,040 ms to set up with platform threads, and +13 MB and 66 ms with virtual threads.

143
00:10:01.422 --> 00:10:07.715
100,000 virtual threads took 519 ms and +170 MB.

144
00:10:07.995 --> 00:10:09.489
How virtual threads work:

145
00:10:09.769 --> 00:10:12.649
A virtual thread is a Continuation on the heap.

146
00:10:12.929 --> 00:10:22.529
When it blocks on a supported operation (socket I or O, Thread.sleep, Lock, BlockingQueue), the JVM saves its stack to the heap and unmounts it.

147
00:10:22.809 --> 00:10:28.633
The carrier thread (from a ForkJoinPool, by default one per core) picks up another virtual thread.

148
00:10:29.983 --> 00:10:34.420
When the I or O completes, the virtual thread is rescheduled on any carrier.

149
00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:38.220
File I or O and some native calls still block the carrier.

150
00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:42.127
The JVM compensates by temporarily adding carriers.

151
00:10:43.477 --> 00:10:45.269
Little's law and pool sizing.

152
00:10:45.549 --> 00:10:53.165
L = λ times W: in-flight requests = arrival rate times time in system (Ch 17).

153
00:10:53.445 --> 00:10:57.071
CPU-bound work: throughput peaks at about the number of cores.

154
00:10:57.351 --> 00:10:59.698
More threads only add context switching.

155
00:10:59.978 --> 00:11:03.562
L1 B (2 cores, 2 ms of CPU per task):

156
00:11:03.842 --> 00:11:13.591
1 thread 489 per second, 2 threads 967 per second, 4 to 128 threads ~1,000 per second.

157
00:11:13.871 --> 00:11:18.757
I or O-bound work: threads about cores times (1 + wait or compute).

158
00:11:19.037 --> 00:11:38.813
For 0.5 ms of CPU and 10 ms of waiting on 2 cores that's 42, and L1 B measured the peak there (3,604 per second versus 1,489 per second at 16 and 3,496 per second at 128).

159
00:11:39.093 --> 00:11:46.090
With virtual threads, you don't size a thread pool for I or O-bound work, because the pool is effectively unbounded.

160
00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:47.415
You still size:

161
00:11:48.765 --> 00:11:54.867
Carrier parallelism (default = cores, jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.parallelism).

162
00:11:55.147 --> 00:12:02.357
Connection pools (Hikari: the database's capacity, often 10 to 50 per service instance, not thousands).

163
00:12:02.637 --> 00:12:06.456
Per-dependency concurrency limits (semaphores, Ch 18).

164
00:12:06.736 --> 00:12:10.213
CPU-bound work separately, on a bounded platform pool.

165
00:12:11.563 --> 00:12:12.289
Pinning.

166
00:12:12.569 --> 00:12:25.326
On JDK 21 to 23, a virtual thread is pinned to its carrier and can't unmount when it blocks: inside a synchronized block or method;. in a native method or foreign function call.

167
00:12:25.606 --> 00:12:31.643
With a few carriers, pinned blocking reduces the whole application's concurrency to the number of carriers.

168
00:12:31.923 --> 00:12:39.006
L2 ran 200 virtual threads that each hold their own lock (no contention) and block for 50 ms:

169
00:12:40.356 --> 00:12:41.700
Here is a quick table:

170
00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:44.753
JDK, and synchronized, and ReentrantLock.

171
00:12:45.033 --> 00:12:46.719
21. synchronized:

172
00:12:46.999 --> 00:12:51.735
5,031 ms (200 times 50 ms or 2 carriers).

173
00:12:52.015 --> 00:12:53.103
ReentrantLock:

174
00:12:53.383 --> 00:12:54.620
53 ms.

175
00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.649
25. synchronized:

176
00:12:56.929 --> 00:12:58.167
54 ms.

177
00:12:58.447 --> 00:12:59.535
ReentrantLock:

178
00:12:59.815 --> 00:13:01.052
53 ms.

179
00:13:02.402 --> 00:13:12.386
JEP 491 (JDK 24) made virtual threads able to unmount while holding monitors, which removes the main source of pinning.

180
00:13:12.666 --> 00:13:14.970
On JDK 21 to 23:

181
00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:25.063
Use -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full (21 to 23) or JFR's jdk.VirtualThreadPinned event to find pinning.

182
00:13:25.343 --> 00:13:28.991
Replace synchronized around blocking calls with ReentrantLock.

183
00:13:29.271 --> 00:13:35.906
Upgrade libraries (JDBC drivers, HTTP clients and logging frameworks fixed many cases).

184
00:13:36.186 --> 00:13:45.381
On any JDK, native calls still pin, and holding any lock while doing I or O is still a contention problem (other threads wait on the lock).

185
00:13:46.731 --> 00:13:49.077
ThreadLocal pitfalls and ScopedValue.

186
00:13:49.357 --> 00:13:54.776
ThreadLocal is used for two different things, and virtual threads break different assumptions for each:

187
00:13:56.126 --> 00:13:57.470
Here is a quick table:

188
00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:00.502
Use, and Old assumption, and With virtual threads.

189
00:14:00.782 --> 00:14:05.475
Per-thread cache of an expensive object (buffers, SimpleDateFormat, parsers).

190
00:14:05.755 --> 00:14:06.801
Old assumption:

191
00:14:07.081 --> 00:14:10.153
A few long-lived pooled threads reuse the object.

192
00:14:10.433 --> 00:14:11.649
With virtual threads:

193
00:14:11.929 --> 00:14:22.446
One virtual thread per request, so one object per request: no reuse, lots of memory (L2: +349 MB at 20,000 in flight).

194
00:14:22.726 --> 00:14:26.331
Request context (user, tenant, trace id, transaction).

195
00:14:26.611 --> 00:14:27.657
Old assumption:

196
00:14:27.937 --> 00:14:30.198
Set at request start, cleared at the end.

197
00:14:30.478 --> 00:14:31.694
With virtual threads:

198
00:14:31.974 --> 00:14:45.563
Still works (virtual threads have ThreadLocals), but must be cleared, isn't inherited by tasks you fork unless using InheritableThreadLocal (which copies, and copies cost memory), and is mutable from anywhere.

199
00:14:46.913 --> 00:14:47.809
Fixes:

200
00:14:48.089 --> 00:14:58.436
Caches: use a proper object pool, immutable thread-safe objects (DateTimeFormatter instead of SimpleDateFormat), or allocate per use (often cheap enough).

201
00:14:59.786 --> 00:15:05.567
Context: use ScopedValue (final in JDK 25, JEP 506).

202
00:15:05.847 --> 00:15:14.957
It's bound for the duration of a scope, immutable, automatically unbound at the end, and inherited by subtasks forked in a StructuredTaskScope.

203
00:15:15.237 --> 00:15:22.149
L2 B showed the request id visible in the handler and in both forked subtasks, and unbound afterwards.

204
00:15:22.429 --> 00:15:25.693
Nothing to clean up, and nothing leaks into the next request.

205
00:15:25.973 --> 00:15:26.954
Frameworks:

206
00:15:27.234 --> 00:15:35.426
Spring's RequestContextHolder and transaction synchronization still use ThreadLocal, which is fine for context in a single thread.

207
00:15:36.776 --> 00:15:44.520
Micrometer tracing context propagates across your own ExecutorService if you wrap it (ContextExecutorService).

208
00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:49.173
With structured concurrency, ensure context propagation into subtasks:

209
00:15:49.453 --> 00:15:51.245
ScopedValue does it natively.

210
00:15:52.595 --> 00:15:54.046
Structured concurrency.

211
00:15:54.326 --> 00:16:04.886
Unstructured concurrency (ExecutorService.submit, CompletableFuture.supplyAsync) creates tasks whose lifetime isn't tied to the code that created them:

212
00:16:05.166 --> 00:16:08.003
A failure in one task doesn't cancel the others.

213
00:16:08.283 --> 00:16:11.035
The caller can return while tasks are still running.

214
00:16:11.315 --> 00:16:14.409
Thread dumps don't show which request a task belongs to.

215
00:16:14.689 --> 00:16:25.910
Structured concurrency (JEP 505, a preview API in JDK 25) treats a group of subtasks as one unit of work, with a lexical scope:

