00:17:14.441 --> 00:17:15.849
OK p99:

233
00:17:16.129 --> 00:17:17.729
184 ms.

234
00:17:18.009 --> 00:17:19.503
Error page p50:

235
00:17:19.783 --> 00:17:20.999
31 ms.

236
00:17:21.279 --> 00:17:23.263
Tasks left running after the reply:

237
00:17:23.543 --> 00:17:24.844
116.

238
00:17:25.124 --> 00:17:26.468
StructuredTaskScope.

239
00:17:26.748 --> 00:17:28.007
OK p50:

240
00:17:28.287 --> 00:17:29.524
41 ms.

241
00:17:29.804 --> 00:17:31.212
OK p99:

242
00:17:31.492 --> 00:17:33.241
152 ms.

243
00:17:33.521 --> 00:17:35.015
Error page p50:

244
00:17:35.295 --> 00:17:36.297
8 ms.

245
00:17:36.577 --> 00:17:38.561
Tasks left running after the reply:

246
00:17:38.841 --> 00:17:39.588
0.

247
00:17:40.938 --> 00:17:44.138
Parallel beats sequential on latency, as expected.

248
00:17:44.418 --> 00:17:46.423
Structured concurrency fails fast.

249
00:17:46.703 --> 00:17:53.722
An inventory failure returns the error page in 8 ms instead of waiting for the siblings (31 ms).

250
00:17:54.002 --> 00:17:58.994
A time budget on the optional scope caps reviews, and nothing outlives the request:

251
00:17:59.274 --> 00:18:07.914
0 tasks left running, versus 116 abandoned futures still consuming threads and connections with CompletableFuture.

252
00:18:09.264 --> 00:18:18.949
What it does not do: the server work after reply column in the output is higher for structured concurrency (276 versus 116).

253
00:18:19.229 --> 00:18:32.264
Cancelling a subtask interrupts our HTTP call, but the remote service keeps working unless the cancellation or deadline propagates to it (Ch 17: deadline headers, server-side checks).

254
00:18:32.544 --> 00:18:35.147
Structured concurrency bounds our resources.

255
00:18:35.427 --> 00:18:37.539
Deadline propagation bounds theirs.

256
00:18:37.819 --> 00:18:38.800
You need both.

257
00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:50.539
In Spring (JDK 25), enable previews for the service (--enable-preview), or keep a thin wrapper so the preview API can be swapped when it's finalized.

258
00:18:50.819 --> 00:19:05.454
Where previews aren't allowed, emulate it: an executor per request in try-with-resources (JDK 21's ExecutorService is AutoCloseable), cancel the remaining futures on the first failure, and put budgets on optional parts.

259
00:19:05.734 --> 00:19:10.171
You lose some guarantees, and it's still far better than fire-and-forget futures.

260
00:19:11.521 --> 00:19:14.359
Reactive programming: when it still earns its place.

261
00:19:14.639 --> 00:19:21.081
With virtual threads, most request or response services no longer need reactive code for scalability.

262
00:19:21.361 --> 00:19:26.353
Reactive (Project Reactor, WebFlux, RxJava) remains a good fit for:

263
00:19:26.633 --> 00:19:27.465
Streaming:

264
00:19:27.745 --> 00:19:36.769
SSE or WebSocket push (live order status, rider location to the app), with operators for merging, windowing and throttling streams.

265
00:19:37.049 --> 00:19:46.841
Backpressure across async boundaries: the Reactive Streams protocol (request(n)) propagates demand end to end (Kafka, then processing, then WebSocket).

266
00:19:48.191 --> 00:19:54.549
Existing reactive stacks and drivers (R2DBC, reactive Mongo, Spring Cloud Gateway).

267
00:19:54.829 --> 00:20:01.826
Costs: a separate programming model, harder debugging, and function colouring (everything in the chain must be non-blocking).

268
00:20:02.106 --> 00:20:06.415
Blocking calls inside reactive pipelines are a classic outage cause.

269
00:20:06.695 --> 00:20:08.274
Use BlockHound in tests.

270
00:20:08.554 --> 00:20:09.599
Platform rule:

271
00:20:09.879 --> 00:20:12.781
Spring MVC + virtual threads by default.

272
00:20:13.061 --> 00:20:17.711
WebFlux for gateways, streaming endpoints and existing reactive services.

273
00:20:19.061 --> 00:20:20.064
Backpressure.

274
00:20:20.344 --> 00:20:23.864
When producers are faster than consumers, something has to give.

275
00:20:24.144 --> 00:20:31.653
The options are to buffer (bounded), slow the producer (block or demand), drop (oldest or newest or sample), or fail.

276
00:20:31.933 --> 00:20:38.675
L4 (GPS pings at 50,000 per second, consumer at ~10,000 per second, 4 s):

277
00:20:40.025 --> 00:20:41.369
Here is a quick table:

278
00:20:41.649 --> 00:20:44.934
Strategy, and Queued at end, and Peak heap, and Max lag.

279
00:20:45.214 --> 00:20:46.345
Unbounded queue.

280
00:20:46.625 --> 00:20:47.627
Queued at end:

281
00:20:47.907 --> 00:20:51.811
165,633 and growing.

282
00:20:52.091 --> 00:20:52.987
Peak heap:

283
00:20:53.267 --> 00:20:55.187
61 MB and growing.

284
00:20:55.467 --> 00:20:56.427
Max lag:

285
00:20:56.707 --> 00:20:59.587
3,312 ms and growing.

286
00:20:59.867 --> 00:21:02.513
Bounded queue (1,000), producer blocks.

287
00:21:02.793 --> 00:21:03.795
Queued at end:

288
00:21:04.075 --> 00:21:05.505
998.

289
00:21:05.785 --> 00:21:06.681
Peak heap:

290
00:21:06.961 --> 00:21:08.049
7 MB.

291
00:21:08.329 --> 00:21:09.289
Max lag:

292
00:21:09.569 --> 00:21:11.361
139 ms.

293
00:21:11.641 --> 00:21:14.201
Flow publisher (demand-driven) + drop.

294
00:21:14.481 --> 00:21:17.083
Queued at end: ~1,000 buffer.

295
00:21:17.363 --> 00:21:18.259
Peak heap:

296
00:21:18.539 --> 00:21:19.777
26 MB.

297
00:21:20.057 --> 00:21:21.017
Max lag:

298
00:21:21.297 --> 00:21:27.462
145 ms, with 161,465 pings dropped.

299
00:21:28.812 --> 00:21:30.241
What the three results show:

300
00:21:30.521 --> 00:21:34.084
The unbounded queue hides the problem until memory runs out.

301
00:21:34.364 --> 00:21:39.100
Meanwhile the data it delivers is seconds old, which is useless for live tracking.

302
00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:48.724
Blocking the producer pushes back upstream: the Kafka consumer polls less, and lag builds in Kafka, where it's durable and visible (Ch 14).

303
00:21:49.004 --> 00:21:50.839
That's the right place for backlog.

304
00:21:51.119 --> 00:21:56.751
Dropping is right when newer data supersedes older data (GPS positions, metrics).

305
00:21:57.031 --> 00:21:58.567
Keep the latest per rider.

306
00:21:59.917 --> 00:22:07.405
For Kafka consumers: max.poll.records, pause or resume of partitions, and bounded in-memory hand-off queues.

307
00:22:07.685 --> 00:22:13.125
For HTTP ingress: bounded concurrency and shedding (Ch 18 L4).

308
00:22:13.405 --> 00:22:14.685
For async pipelines:

309
00:22:14.965 --> 00:22:17.653
Reactive Streams demand, or bounded channels.

310
00:22:19.003 --> 00:22:21.712
Shared state, contention and the memory model.

311
00:22:21.992 --> 00:22:26.472
With massive concurrency, contention on shared state becomes the bottleneck:

312
00:22:26.752 --> 00:22:41.685
Visibility and ordering: the Java Memory Model guarantees visibility only across happens-before edges: volatile writes or reads, lock release or acquire, thread start or join, and java.util.concurrent hand-offs.

313
00:22:41.965 --> 00:22:45.741
A plain field written by one thread may never be seen by another.

314
00:22:46.021 --> 00:22:51.376
Atomicity: use AtomicLong or AtomicReference (CAS) for single variables.

315
00:22:51.656 --> 00:22:56.627
Use LongAdder for high-contention counters: it stripes the updates and sums on read.

316
00:22:56.907 --> 00:22:59.957
Use ConcurrentHashMap.compute for per-key updates.

317
00:23:01.307 --> 00:23:11.142
Locks: keep critical sections short, never do I or O while holding a lock, and prefer lock-free or immutable data (records, persistent collections).

318
00:23:11.422 --> 00:23:15.518
Per-request state stays per request: the safest concurrency is none.

319
00:23:15.798 --> 00:23:23.990
Most service code should be stateless (Ch 09), with state in databases and caches that have their own concurrency control.

320
00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:27.431
Diagnosing concurrency problems.

321
00:23:27.711 --> 00:23:37.503
Thread dumps: jcmd pid Thread.dumptofile -format=json includes virtual threads (millions of them are summarized, grouped by stack).

322
00:23:37.783 --> 00:23:40.727
Look for many threads waiting on the same lock or pool.

323
00:23:41.007 --> 00:23:54.575
JFR: jdk.VirtualThreadPinned, jdk.VirtualThreadSubmitFailed, lock contention (jdk.JavaMonitorEnter), socket reads with durations, and jdk.ThreadSleep.

324
00:23:54.855 --> 00:24:03.729
Metrics: in-flight requests, pool utilization (Hikari active or pending), semaphore permits, queue depths, and carrier CPU usage.

325
00:24:05.079 --> 00:24:09.858
Load tests at and beyond capacity with realistic dependency latencies.

326
00:24:10.138 --> 00:24:13.765
L2's pinning only shows up when blocking happens under load.

