667
00:50:07.809 --> 00:50:09.089
Production Concerns.

668
00:50:10.339 --> 00:50:11.491
Failure modes.

669
00:50:11.771 --> 00:50:13.115
Here is a quick table:

670
00:50:13.395 --> 00:50:16.381
Failure, and Symptom, and Cause, and Mitigation.

671
00:50:16.661 --> 00:50:18.432
Carrier starvation (pinning).

672
00:50:18.712 --> 00:50:26.413
Symptom: p99 explodes under load, low CPU, thread dump shows carriers inside synchronized or native.

673
00:50:26.693 --> 00:50:27.461
Cause:

674
00:50:27.741 --> 00:50:34.099
Blocking while pinned (JDK 21 to 23 monitors, JNI, some file I or O).

675
00:50:34.379 --> 00:50:35.381
Mitigation:

676
00:50:35.661 --> 00:50:43.192
JDK 24 plus, ReentrantLock around I or O, JFR jdk.VirtualThreadPinned alerts.

677
00:50:43.472 --> 00:50:44.731
Downstream flooded.

678
00:50:45.011 --> 00:50:45.821
Symptom:

679
00:50:46.101 --> 00:50:51.285
Dependency p99 and errors rise right after the virtual-thread rollout.

680
00:50:51.565 --> 00:50:52.333
Cause:

681
00:50:52.613 --> 00:50:56.027
The old 200-thread ceiling was an accidental limit.

682
00:50:56.307 --> 00:50:57.309
Mitigation:

683
00:50:57.589 --> 00:51:01.237
Semaphore per dependency, adaptive limits (Ch 18).

684
00:51:01.517 --> 00:51:03.139
Connection pool exhaustion.

685
00:51:03.419 --> 00:51:04.229
Symptom:

686
00:51:04.509 --> 00:51:08.221
HikariPool - Connection is not available, request timed out.

687
00:51:08.501 --> 00:51:09.269
Cause:

688
00:51:09.549 --> 00:51:13.133
Thousands of virtual threads contending for 10 connections.

689
00:51:13.413 --> 00:51:14.416
Mitigation:

690
00:51:14.696 --> 00:51:20.605
Accept it as the real limit: short timeouts, shed early, never size the pool to the thread count.

691
00:51:21.955 --> 00:51:22.915
Heap growth.

692
00:51:23.195 --> 00:51:24.006
Symptom:

693
00:51:24.286 --> 00:51:27.294
Heap scales with in-flight requests, GC pressure.

694
00:51:27.574 --> 00:51:28.342
Cause:

695
00:51:28.622 --> 00:51:33.763
ThreadLocal caches, InheritableThreadLocal copies, large per-request buffers.

696
00:51:34.043 --> 00:51:35.046
Mitigation:

697
00:51:35.326 --> 00:51:39.017
Pools, ScopedValue, heap-per-request budget in load tests.

698
00:51:39.297 --> 00:51:40.491
Leaked async work.

699
00:51:40.771 --> 00:51:41.582
Symptom:

700
00:51:41.862 --> 00:51:45.467
Threads and connections busy after the responses were sent.

701
00:51:45.747 --> 00:51:46.515
Cause:

702
00:51:46.795 --> 00:51:48.929
Fire-and-forget CompletableFutures.

703
00:51:49.209 --> 00:51:50.211
Mitigation:

704
00:51:50.491 --> 00:51:53.947
Structured concurrency, cancellation on failure (L3).

705
00:51:54.227 --> 00:51:55.059
Deadlock.

706
00:51:55.339 --> 00:51:56.150
Symptom:

707
00:51:56.430 --> 00:51:59.502
Requests hang forever, thread dump shows a lock cycle.

708
00:51:59.782 --> 00:52:00.550
Cause:

709
00:52:00.830 --> 00:52:04.435
Inconsistent lock order, nested pools waiting on themselves.

710
00:52:04.715 --> 00:52:05.718
Mitigation:

711
00:52:05.998 --> 00:52:11.502
Lock ordering, no blocking on the same bounded pool from inside it, tryLock with timeout.

712
00:52:12.852 --> 00:52:13.983
Unbounded queue.

713
00:52:14.263 --> 00:52:15.073
Symptom:

714
00:52:15.353 --> 00:52:17.316
Rising latency then OOM.

715
00:52:17.596 --> 00:52:18.364
Cause:

716
00:52:18.644 --> 00:52:21.076
Producer faster than consumer (L4).

717
00:52:21.356 --> 00:52:22.359
Mitigation:

718
00:52:22.639 --> 00:52:26.649
Bounded queues with an explicit reject or drop or block policy.

719
00:52:26.929 --> 00:52:28.359
Blocking in an event loop.

720
00:52:28.639 --> 00:52:29.449
Symptom:

721
00:52:29.729 --> 00:52:32.439
Reactive service freezes (all requests stall).

722
00:52:32.719 --> 00:52:33.487
Cause:

723
00:52:33.767 --> 00:52:36.775
A JDBC call or block() on a Netty thread.

724
00:52:37.055 --> 00:52:38.057
Mitigation:

725
00:52:38.337 --> 00:52:42.988
BlockHound in tests, publishOn(boundedElastic) for unavoidable blocking.

726
00:52:43.268 --> 00:52:45.273
CPU work on virtual threads.

727
00:52:45.553 --> 00:52:46.364
Symptom:

728
00:52:46.644 --> 00:52:50.612
Latency of I or O requests rises when CPU jobs run.

729
00:52:50.892 --> 00:52:51.660
Cause:

730
00:52:51.940 --> 00:52:55.012
Long CPU tasks monopolize the few carriers.

731
00:52:55.292 --> 00:52:56.295
Mitigation:

732
00:52:56.575 --> 00:52:59.668
Separate bounded platform pool for CPU-heavy work.

733
00:53:01.018 --> 00:53:02.106
Observability.

734
00:53:02.386 --> 00:53:08.871
RED plus concurrency metrics: in-flight requests (the L in Little's law), arrival rate, latency.

735
00:53:09.151 --> 00:53:12.735
If in-flight grows while throughput stays flat, you're queueing somewhere.

736
00:53:13.015 --> 00:53:14.189
Resource gauges:

737
00:53:14.469 --> 00:53:22.895
Hikari active or idle or pending, semaphore available permits, executor queue depth and active count, carrier-pool CPU.

738
00:53:23.175 --> 00:53:38.087
JFR continuous recording in production (~1 % overhead) with jdk.VirtualThreadPinned (threshold 20 ms), jdk.JavaMonitorEnter, jdk.SocketRead, and allocation profiling.

739
00:53:38.367 --> 00:53:39.605
Dump it on alerts.

740
00:53:40.955 --> 00:53:49.659
Thread dumps with virtual threads: jcmd pid Thread.dumptofile -format=json or tmp or threads.json.

741
00:53:49.939 --> 00:53:54.909
Group by stack to see 4,000 threads waiting on HikariPool.getConnection.

742
00:53:55.189 --> 00:54:04.683
Tracing: context propagated into subtasks (ScopedValue or context-aware executors), so spans from a fan-out attach to the right parent.

743
00:54:06.033 --> 00:54:06.971
Security.

744
00:54:07.251 --> 00:54:17.321
Context leaks between requests are a security bug: a ThreadLocal user or tenant not cleared on a pooled thread shows customer A's data to customer B.

745
00:54:17.601 --> 00:54:20.843
ScopedValue bounds make that structurally impossible.

746
00:54:21.123 --> 00:54:24.089
Unbounded concurrency is a DoS amplifier.

747
00:54:24.369 --> 00:54:33.243
With virtual threads, one slow-loris client can hold 100,000 cheap threads, each holding heavier resources (buffers, connections).

748
00:54:33.523 --> 00:54:38.067
Keep server limits on connections, header or body sizes and read timeouts.

749
00:54:39.417 --> 00:54:41.359
Security context propagation:

750
00:54:41.639 --> 00:54:45.628
Spring Security's SecurityContextHolder is ThreadLocal-based.

751
00:54:45.908 --> 00:54:55.636
Use DelegatingSecurityContextExecutor or pass the principal explicitly into subtasks, and never let subtasks run with an empty or wrong principal.

752
00:54:56.986 --> 00:54:58.650
Performance and tuning knobs.

753
00:54:58.930 --> 00:55:00.274
Here is a quick table:

754
00:55:00.554 --> 00:55:05.397
Knob, and Default, and Guidance. spring.threads.virtual.enabled (VERIFY).

755
00:55:05.677 --> 00:55:06.871
Default: false.

756
00:55:07.151 --> 00:55:15.301
Guidance: true on JDK 21 plus, best on 24 plus. jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.parallelism.

757
00:55:15.581 --> 00:55:16.775
Default: cores.

758
00:55:17.055 --> 00:55:17.951
Guidance:

759
00:55:18.231 --> 00:55:27.639
Leave it; set container CPU limits correctly (the JVM is container-aware). jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.maxPoolSize.

760
00:55:27.919 --> 00:55:28.751
Default:

761
00:55:29.031 --> 00:55:30.418
256.

762
00:55:30.698 --> 00:55:31.594
Guidance:

763
00:55:31.874 --> 00:55:35.437
Extra carriers to compensate for pinning or file I or O.

764
00:55:35.717 --> 00:55:38.127
Tomcat server.tomcat.threads.max.

765
00:55:38.407 --> 00:55:39.239
Default:

766
00:55:39.519 --> 00:55:40.437
200.

767
00:55:40.717 --> 00:55:41.613
Guidance:

768
00:55:41.893 --> 00:55:47.482
Irrelevant with virtual threads; use server.tomcat.max-connections and limits instead.

769
00:55:47.762 --> 00:55:49.661
Hikari maximumPoolSize.

770
00:55:49.941 --> 00:55:50.773
Default:

771
00:55:51.053 --> 00:55:51.757
10.

772
00:55:52.037 --> 00:55:52.933
Guidance:

773
00:55:53.213 --> 00:55:57.074
From DB capacity ÷ instances, not from threads.

774
00:55:57.354 --> 00:55:58.847
CPU pool size.

775
00:55:59.127 --> 00:56:03.266
Guidance: availableProcessors(), bounded queue, rejection policy.

776
00:56:04.616 --> 00:56:06.195
Semaphore per dependency.

777
00:56:06.475 --> 00:56:15.669
Guidance: ~2 to 3x expected in-flight (Little's law) at normal latency. -XX:+UseZGC or G1.

778
00:56:15.949 --> 00:56:16.781
Default:

779
00:56:17.061 --> 00:56:17.957
G1.

780
00:56:18.237 --> 00:56:19.133
Guidance:

781
00:56:19.413 --> 00:56:28.907
Many short-lived virtual threads are allocation-heavy; generational ZGC (JDK 21 plus) for low pauses at large heaps.

782
00:56:30.257 --> 00:56:30.982
Cost.

783
00:56:31.262 --> 00:56:42.590
Moving an I or O-bound service from 200-thread pools to virtual threads typically lets each pod carry more concurrent requests at the same memory, so fewer pods are needed.

784
00:56:42.870 --> 00:56:47.414
Measure it, because CPU and dependencies usually become the new ceiling.

785
00:56:47.694 --> 00:56:59.961
The migration cost is mostly auditing: synchronized + I or O (on JDK 21 to 23), ThreadLocal caches, native libraries, and adding the missing explicit limits.

786
00:57:00.241 --> 00:57:05.489
The platform wrapper (ADR-079) contains the preview-API risk.

787
00:57:05.769 --> 00:57:08.926
Reactive rewrites are expensive in training and maintenance.

788
00:57:09.206 --> 00:57:11.702
Reserve them for streaming, where they pay back.

789
00:57:13.052 --> 00:57:13.991
Part 7.

790
00:57:14.271 --> 00:57:15.977
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

791
00:57:17.227 --> 00:57:17.867
1.

792
00:57:18.147 --> 00:57:23.203
Blocking I or O inside synchronized on JDK 21 to 23.

793
00:57:24.553 --> 00:57:30.036
Here is the Java code. before: pins the carrier on JDK 21 (L2:

794
00:57:30.316 --> 00:57:34.433
5,031 ms versus 53 ms). after:

795
00:57:34.713 --> 00:57:41.284
ReentrantLock (or no lock at all: most clients are thread-safe), or run on JDK 24 plus.

796
00:57:44.134 --> 00:57:44.838
2.

797
00:57:45.118 --> 00:57:46.547
Pooling virtual threads.

798
00:57:47.897 --> 00:57:57.881
Here is the Java code. before: a pool of virtual threads, used as a concurrency limit. after: a thread per task, and limit the RESOURCE explicitly.

799
00:58:00.731 --> 00:58:03.163
Virtual threads are cheap and disposable.

800
00:58:03.443 --> 00:58:07.539
Limit what's scarce (connections, dependency capacity), not threads.

801
00:58:07.819 --> 00:58:08.566
3.

802
00:58:08.846 --> 00:58:14.670
ThreadLocal as a cache of expensive objects (L2: +349 MB).

803
00:58:16.020 --> 00:58:20.457
Here is the Java code. after: immutable, thread-safe, one instance.

804
00:58:23.307 --> 00:58:24.011
4.

805
00:58:24.291 --> 00:58:27.449
Fire-and-forget CompletableFuture fan-out (L3:

806
00:58:27.729 --> 00:58:31.526
116 tasks leaked, 31 ms error pages).

807
00:58:32.876 --> 00:58:37.633
Here is the Java code. after: structured, fail-fast, bounded lifetime.

808
00:58:40.483 --> 00:58:41.251
5.

809
00:58:41.531 --> 00:58:46.587
Unbounded queues between stages (L4: lag 3.3 s and growing).

810
00:58:47.937 --> 00:58:52.737
Here is the Java code. after: bounded, with an explicit policy when full.

811
00:58:55.587 --> 00:58:56.334
6.

812
00:58:56.614 --> 00:58:59.387
CPU-heavy work on the virtual-thread carriers.

813
00:59:00.737 --> 00:59:12.065
Here is the Java code. before: image resizing in the request's virtual thread starves the few carriers. after: a bounded platform pool sized to cores, with backpressure.

814
00:59:14.915 --> 00:59:15.662
7.

815
00:59:15.942 --> 00:59:18.225
Sizing the DB pool to the thread count.

816
00:59:19.575 --> 00:59:33.057
Here is the PROPERTIES code. before: we have 5,000 virtual threads, so give Hikari 1,000 connections, then 20 pods times 1,000 = 20,000 connections to Postgres. after:

817
00:59:33.337 --> 00:59:39.247
Little's law on the query side, and the database's total connection budget ÷ instances.

818
00:59:42.097 --> 00:59:42.779
8.

819
00:59:43.059 --> 00:59:45.171
Blocking inside a reactive pipeline.

820
00:59:46.521 --> 00:59:48.527
Here is the Java code. before:

821
00:59:48.807 --> 01:00:01.116
JDBC on a Netty event-loop thread freezes every connection on that loop. after: isolate it (or use R2DBC, or move the endpoint to MVC + virtual threads).

822
01:00:03.966 --> 01:00:04.713
9.

823
01:00:04.993 --> 01:00:07.979
Forgetting to clear request context on pooled threads.

824
01:00:09.329 --> 01:00:17.991
Here is the Java code. before: the next request on this pooled thread sees the previous tenant. after: scoped binding, nothing to clear.

825
01:00:20.841 --> 01:00:21.545
10.

826
01:00:21.825 --> 01:00:24.299
Believing virtual threads make code faster.

827
01:00:24.579 --> 01:00:26.286
They reduce the cost of waiting.

828
01:00:26.566 --> 01:00:37.403
CPU-bound latency is unchanged (L1 B), and a service bottlenecked on the database gets no faster, it just queues on the connection pool instead of the thread pool.

829
01:00:37.683 --> 01:00:39.390
Measure where time is spent first.

830
01:00:39.670 --> 01:00:40.502
11.

831
01:00:40.782 --> 01:00:43.257
Shared mutable state without happens-before.

832
01:00:44.607 --> 01:00:50.793
Here is the Java code. before: a plain boolean flag may never become visible to the worker thread.

833
01:00:53.643 --> 01:00:54.518
Part 8.

834
01:00:54.798 --> 01:00:56.142
Interview Q and A.

835
01:00:57.392 --> 01:00:58.224
Beginner.

836
01:00:58.504 --> 01:00:59.528
Question 1.

837
01:00:59.808 --> 01:01:04.331
What is the difference between a process and a thread, and why do servers use threads?

838
01:01:04.611 --> 01:01:05.976
Thirty-second answer:

839
01:01:06.256 --> 01:01:08.432
A process has its own memory space.

840
01:01:08.712 --> 01:01:12.979
Threads are units of execution inside a process that share its memory.

841
01:01:13.259 --> 01:01:22.496
Servers use threads so many requests can be handled at the same time, and while one request waits on a database or network call, others can make progress.

842
01:01:23.846 --> 01:01:24.849
Question 2.

843
01:01:25.129 --> 01:01:26.451
What are virtual threads?

844
01:01:26.731 --> 01:01:28.097
Thirty-second answer:

845
01:01:28.377 --> 01:01:34.350
Lightweight threads (JDK 21 plus) managed by the JVM rather than the OS.

846
01:01:34.630 --> 01:01:42.737
When a virtual thread blocks on I or O, the JVM parks it and frees the underlying OS thread (carrier) to run another one.

847
01:01:43.017 --> 01:01:49.587
You can have hundreds of thousands, so you can keep writing simple blocking code and still handle huge concurrency.

848
01:01:50.937 --> 01:01:51.983
Question 3.

849
01:01:52.263 --> 01:01:53.649
What is a race condition?

850
01:01:53.929 --> 01:01:55.017
Give an example.

851
01:01:55.297 --> 01:01:56.663
Thirty-second answer:

852
01:01:56.943 --> 01:01:59.844
A bug where the result depends on the timing of threads.

853
01:02:00.124 --> 01:02:03.409
Example: two threads do count++ on the same field.

854
01:02:03.689 --> 01:02:08.788
It's a read-modify-write, so both can read 5 and write 6, losing an increment.

855
01:02:10.138 --> 01:02:11.205
Question 4.

856
01:02:11.485 --> 01:02:13.618
Why is an unbounded queue dangerous?

857
01:02:13.898 --> 01:02:15.263
Thirty-second answer:

858
01:02:15.543 --> 01:02:23.309
If producers are faster than consumers, it grows until memory runs out, and before that every item waits longer and longer.

859
01:02:23.589 --> 01:02:34.127
L4: after 4 s of overload, 165,633 items were queued and the consumer was processing data 3.3 s old.

860
01:02:35.477 --> 01:02:36.480
Intermediate.

861
01:02:36.760 --> 01:02:37.869
Question 5.

862
01:02:38.149 --> 01:02:39.771
How do you size a thread pool?

863
01:02:40.051 --> 01:02:41.416
Thirty-second answer:

864
01:02:41.696 --> 01:02:44.597
For CPU-bound work, about the number of cores.

865
01:02:44.877 --> 01:02:56.227
For work that waits, cores times (1 + wait or compute), and then verify with a load test, because the real limits are usually downstream (connections, dependency capacity).

866
01:02:56.507 --> 01:02:57.339
L1:

867
01:02:57.619 --> 01:03:02.717
CPU-bound peaked at 2 threads on 2 cores (967 per second).

868
01:03:02.997 --> 01:03:16.907
I or O-heavy (0.5 ms CPU + 10 ms wait) peaked at the formula's 42 (3,604 per second versus 1,489 per second at 16).

869
01:03:18.257 --> 01:03:19.366
Question 6.

870
01:03:19.646 --> 01:03:21.694
What is pinning, and how do you avoid it?

871
01:03:21.974 --> 01:03:23.339
Thirty-second answer:

872
01:03:23.619 --> 01:03:27.843
A virtual thread is pinned when it can't unmount from its carrier while blocked.

873
01:03:28.123 --> 01:03:34.075
On JDK 21 to 23 that happens inside synchronized and in native calls.

874
01:03:34.355 --> 01:03:40.883
With only a few carriers, a pinned blocking call blocks a whole carrier, so concurrency collapses to the core count.

875
01:03:41.163 --> 01:03:41.974
L2:

876
01:03:42.254 --> 01:03:55.907
200 times 50 ms of blocking inside synchronized took 5,031 ms on JDK 21, 54 ms on JDK 25, and 53 ms with ReentrantLock.

877
01:03:57.257 --> 01:03:58.345
Question 7.

878
01:03:58.625 --> 01:04:01.911
Why does ThreadLocal behave differently with virtual threads?

879
01:04:02.191 --> 01:04:03.556
Thirty-second answer:

880
01:04:03.836 --> 01:04:12.071
ThreadLocals still work, but a common idiom breaks: caching an expensive object per thread assumes a few long-lived pooled threads.

881
01:04:12.351 --> 01:04:16.553
With a virtual thread per request, every request creates its own copy.

882
01:04:16.833 --> 01:04:27.372
L2: a 16 KB buffer per thread cost 1 MB with 200 pooled threads and 349 MB with 20,000 virtual threads in flight.

883
01:04:28.722 --> 01:04:29.746
Question 8.

884
01:04:30.026 --> 01:04:33.482
How would you call four services in parallel for a product page?

885
01:04:33.762 --> 01:04:35.127
Thirty-second answer:

886
01:04:35.407 --> 01:04:38.586
Fork the calls concurrently with a time budget per part.

887
01:04:38.866 --> 01:04:47.207
Required parts (catalog, price, stock) fail the page fast if any fails; optional parts (reviews) get a short budget and a fallback.

888
01:04:47.487 --> 01:04:53.290
Use structured concurrency so the first failure cancels the siblings and nothing outlives the request.

889
01:04:53.570 --> 01:04:54.423
L3:

890
01:04:54.703 --> 01:05:07.759
41 ms p50 versus 124 ms sequential, 8 ms error pages versus 31 ms with CompletableFuture, and 0 leaked tasks versus 116.

891
01:05:09.109 --> 01:05:10.176
Question 9.

892
01:05:10.456 --> 01:05:13.933
What is the Java Memory Model's happens-before, in practice?

893
01:05:14.213 --> 01:05:15.579
Thirty-second answer:

894
01:05:15.859 --> 01:05:21.299
A guarantee that writes made before one action are visible to code after another action.

895
01:05:21.579 --> 01:05:33.824
The common edges: unlocking a lock and a later lock of it, a volatile write and a later read of it, Thread.start or join, and hand-offs through java.util.concurrent (queues, futures, latches).

896
01:05:34.104 --> 01:05:38.669
Without such an edge, another thread may never see your write, or see it out of order.

897
01:05:40.019 --> 01:05:40.787
Senior.

898
01:05:41.067 --> 01:05:42.113
Question 10.

899
01:05:42.393 --> 01:05:45.763
What is structured concurrency, and what problems does it solve?

900
01:05:46.043 --> 01:05:47.409
Thirty-second answer:

901
01:05:47.689 --> 01:06:00.083
Treating the subtasks a piece of code starts as one unit with a lexical scope: they're forked inside a block, the block waits for them, a failure or timeout cancels the siblings, and nothing survives the block.

902
01:06:00.363 --> 01:06:05.739
It fixes leaked tasks, lost errors, missing cancellation and unreadable thread dumps.

903
01:06:06.019 --> 01:06:12.163
In Java it's StructuredTaskScope (preview in JDK 25, JEP 505).

904
01:06:13.513 --> 01:06:14.644
Question 11.

905
01:06:14.924 --> 01:06:18.807
What changed when you switched to virtual threads, beyond the config flag?

906
01:06:19.087 --> 01:06:20.452
Thirty-second answer:

907
01:06:20.732 --> 01:06:35.431
Four things: pinning (on JDK 21 to 23), ThreadLocal caches, the loss of the implicit concurrency limit that the thread pool provided, and the realization that connection pools and dependencies are now the bottleneck.

908
01:06:35.711 --> 01:06:36.692
The fixes:

909
01:06:36.972 --> 01:06:47.745
JDK 24 plus or ReentrantLock, pools or ScopedValue, explicit semaphores per dependency, and pool sizes derived from downstream capacity.

910
01:06:49.095 --> 01:06:50.183
Question 12.

911
01:06:50.463 --> 01:06:53.237
Virtual threads or WebFlux for a new service?

912
01:06:53.517 --> 01:06:54.882
Thirty-second answer:

913
01:06:55.162 --> 01:07:05.701
Default to Spring MVC with virtual threads: blocking code that's easy to write, debug and profile, with comparable concurrency for request or response workloads.

914
01:07:05.981 --> 01:07:15.325
Choose WebFlux for streaming (SSE or WebSocket), gateways, backpressure across async boundaries, or when the team and stack are already reactive.

915
01:07:16.675 --> 01:07:17.955
Question 13.

916
01:07:18.235 --> 01:07:21.264
How do you apply backpressure in a Kafka-consuming service?

917
01:07:21.544 --> 01:07:22.909
Thirty-second answer:

918
01:07:23.189 --> 01:07:25.131
Don't pull faster than you can process.

919
01:07:25.411 --> 01:07:36.803
Keep in-memory hand-off queues bounded, pause partitions when full and resume when drained, and tune max.poll.records so a batch finishes within max.poll.interval.ms.

920
01:07:37.083 --> 01:07:42.587
The backlog then stays in Kafka as consumer lag, durable and visible, rather than in the heap.

921
01:07:42.867 --> 01:07:46.365
Scale out with more partitions and consumers when lag grows.

922
01:07:47.715 --> 01:07:48.910
Question 14.

923
01:07:49.190 --> 01:07:54.011
How do you diagnose a Java service that is slow under load but has low CPU?

924
01:07:54.291 --> 01:07:55.657
Thirty-second answer:

925
01:07:55.937 --> 01:08:00.267
Low CPU with high latency means requests are waiting on something.

926
01:08:00.547 --> 01:08:13.795
Take thread dumps (including virtual threads, grouped by stack) and a JFR recording, and look for the common wait: a connection pool, a lock, a semaphore, a pinned carrier, a slow dependency, or GC pauses.

927
01:08:15.145 --> 01:08:16.041
Architect.

928
01:08:16.321 --> 01:08:17.516
Question 15.

929
01:08:17.796 --> 01:08:22.191
Design the concurrency model for the grocery platform's Java services.

930
01:08:23.541 --> 01:08:24.735
Question 16.

931
01:08:25.015 --> 01:08:29.346
Cancelling a subtask in structured concurrency: what does it actually stop?

932
01:08:29.626 --> 01:08:30.991
Thirty-second answer:

933
01:08:31.271 --> 01:08:39.442
It interrupts the subtask's thread, so our blocking HTTP call returns and our resources (thread, connection slot) are freed.

934
01:08:39.722 --> 01:08:41.877
It doesn't stop the remote service's work.

935
01:08:42.157 --> 01:08:51.821
L3 showed 0 client tasks alive after the response, but 276 server-side handlers still completed after the client had moved on.

936
01:08:53.171 --> 01:08:54.515
Question 17.

937
01:08:54.795 --> 01:08:57.909
