940
01:09:05.089 --> 01:09:06.454
Thirty-second answer:

941
01:09:06.734 --> 01:09:08.270
Make capacity explicit:

942
01:09:09.620 --> 01:09:10.857
Question 19.

943
01:09:11.137 --> 01:09:18.476
How would you migrate a large codebase from Java 17 thread pools to Java 25 virtual threads safely?

944
01:09:19.826 --> 01:09:20.935
Question 20.

945
01:09:21.215 --> 01:09:24.223
Where does Little's law show up in your architecture decisions?

946
01:09:24.503 --> 01:09:25.869
Thirty-second answer:

947
01:09:26.149 --> 01:09:28.090
Everywhere concurrency is bounded.

948
01:09:28.370 --> 01:09:39.485
In-flight = arrival rate times latency, so each limit implies a maximum throughput at a given latency, and latency increases raise concurrency, which hits those limits.

949
01:09:39.765 --> 01:09:47.210
Thread pools, connection pools, semaphores, Kafka consumers and queue sizes all fall out of L = λW.

950
01:09:48.560 --> 01:09:49.456
Part 9.

951
01:09:49.736 --> 01:09:51.144
Summary and Practice.

952
01:09:52.394 --> 01:09:53.482
Key takeaways.

953
01:09:53.762 --> 01:09:56.023
Concurrency is capacity allocation.

954
01:09:56.303 --> 01:10:01.210
Every in-flight request holds resources, and Little's law sets how many are in flight.

955
01:10:01.490 --> 01:10:12.477
Virtual threads make waiting cheap (L1: +13 MB for 5,000 versus +151 MB; 100,000 in 170 MB).

956
01:10:12.757 --> 01:10:14.698
They don't make CPU work faster.

957
01:10:14.978 --> 01:10:24.450
Pool sizing: cores for CPU-bound work, cores times (1 + W or C) for I or O-bound pools (L1: peak at 42).

958
01:10:24.730 --> 01:10:28.869
With virtual threads, size connections and dependency limits instead.

959
01:10:29.149 --> 01:10:42.866
Pinning in synchronized + blocking made L2 100x slower on JDK 21 (5,031 versus 53 ms), and JDK 24 plus fixes it (54 ms).

960
01:10:43.146 --> 01:10:44.639
Native calls still pin.

961
01:10:45.989 --> 01:10:51.685
ThreadLocal caches multiply by in-flight requests (+349 MB).

962
01:10:51.965 --> 01:10:55.763
Use pools, immutable objects and ScopedValue for context.

963
01:10:56.043 --> 01:11:08.757
Structured concurrency makes fan-out fail fast (8 ms versus 31 ms), bounded (p99 152 ms) and leak-free (0 versus 116 tasks).

964
01:11:09.037 --> 01:11:10.531
Cancellation is local.

965
01:11:10.811 --> 01:11:18.469
Remote work stops only with deadline propagation (276 server handlers ran on after the reply).

966
01:11:18.749 --> 01:11:20.157
Keep explicit limits.

967
01:11:20.437 --> 01:11:22.080
The thread pool used to be one.

968
01:11:22.360 --> 01:11:25.987
Add semaphores per dependency and bounded CPU pools.

969
01:11:27.337 --> 01:11:32.265
Backpressure: bound every queue and choose block, reject or drop deliberately (L4:

970
01:11:32.545 --> 01:11:37.174
3.3 s lag unbounded versus ~140 ms).

971
01:11:37.454 --> 01:11:39.587
Reactive is for streaming and gateways.

972
01:11:39.867 --> 01:11:42.555
MVC with virtual threads is the default.

973
